WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.137 --> 00:00:03.180
The second case is infinity over infinity.

2
00:00:03.337 --> 00:00:05.655
This does not automatically equal one.

3
00:00:05.769 --> 00:00:08.300
Consider a quotient of polynomials.

4
00:00:08.479 --> 00:00:10.783
Find the limit of three x squared

5
00:00:10.783 --> 00:00:13.677
plus two x, over x squared minus one,

6
00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:16.019
as x tends to positive infinity.

7
00:00:16.151 --> 00:00:18.717
Both numerator and denominator grow without bound.

8
00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:21.690
Divide both by x squared.

9
00:00:21.826 --> 00:00:24.944
The numerator becomes three plus two over x.

10
00:00:25.134 --> 00:00:28.341
The denominator becomes one minus one over x squared.

11
00:00:28.505 --> 00:00:31.065
As x grows, two over x tends

12
00:00:31.065 --> 00:00:33.753
to zero, and one over x squared

13
00:00:33.753 --> 00:00:36.124
also tends to zero. Three over one remains.

14
00:00:36.293 --> 00:00:38.020
Therefore the limit is three.

15
00:00:38.298 --> 00:00:41.234
For this quotient of polynomials,

16
00:00:41.285 --> 00:00:43.468
the highest power of x is useful.

17
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.265
Do not cancel terms in a sum

18
00:00:46.265 --> 00:00:47.708
as though they were factors in a product.
